## Category 11: Operations Anti-Patterns

### 11.1 Logging Anti-Patterns (CRIT)

**CRITICAL:** Avoid over-logging, under-logging, and log injection vulnerabilities.

❌ Bad: Excessive logging in hot paths, missing structured logs, user input in log messages, no log redaction
✅ Good: Structured logging (JSON), log levels appropriate to context, redact sensitive data, log only necessary information
⚠️ Why: Over-logging wastes resources, under-logging hides issues, log injection creates security risks
🔧 Fix: Use structured logging; redact sensitive data; log at appropriate levels; validate log inputs

**Detect:** Excessive logging in loops, missing structured logs, user input in logs, no log redaction, log injection vulnerabilities

---

### 11.2 Metrics Anti-Patterns (CRIT)

**CRITICAL:** Use correct metric types and include necessary labels.

❌ Bad: Wrong metric types (gauge vs counter), missing labels, no metric collection, metrics without context
✅ Good: Correct metric types (counter/gauge/histogram), appropriate labels, consistent metric naming, metrics with context
⚠️ Why: Wrong types give incorrect insights, missing labels prevent filtering, no metrics means no observability
🔧 Fix: Use correct metric types; include necessary labels; follow naming conventions; collect metrics consistently

**Detect:** Wrong metric types, missing labels, no metric collection, metrics without context, inconsistent naming

---

### 11.3 Error Tracking Anti-Patterns (CRIT)

**CRITICAL:** Provide error context and avoid generic error messages.

❌ Bad: Generic error messages, missing stack traces, no error context, errors without correlation IDs
✅ Good: Specific error messages with context, stack traces included, correlation IDs for tracing, error categorization
⚠️ Why: Generic errors are hard to debug, missing context delays resolution, no correlation prevents tracing
🔧 Fix: Include error context; add stack traces; use correlation IDs; categorize errors appropriately

**Detect:** Generic error messages, missing stack traces, no error context, errors without correlation IDs, untraceable errors

---

### 11.4 Observability Blindness (CRIT)

**CRITICAL:** Implement monitoring, alerting, and observability for production systems.

❌ Bad: No monitoring, missing alerts, no observability, no health checks, no performance tracking
✅ Good: Comprehensive monitoring, appropriate alerts, observability tools, health checks, performance metrics
⚠️ Why: No observability means blind operations, missing alerts delay incident response, no health checks prevent proactive detection
🔧 Fix: Implement monitoring; configure alerts; use observability tools; add health checks; track performance metrics

**Detect:** No monitoring, missing alerts, no observability tools, no health checks, no performance tracking

